<p id="mwDA"><i id="mwDQ"><b id="mwDg">Fagus sylvatica</b></i>, the <b id="mwDw">European beech</b> or <b id="mwEA">common beech</b>, is a large <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Deciduous" title="Deciduous" id="mwEQ">deciduous</a> tree in the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fagaceae" title="Fagaceae" id="mwEg">beech</a> family with smooth silvery-grey bark, large leaf area, and a short trunk with low branches.<sup about="#mwt19" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-3" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-3"}}'><a href="#cite_note-3" id="mwEw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwFA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwFQ">[</span>3<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwFg">]</span></span></a></sup> It is native to much of Europe, growing in humid climates.</p>

<p id="mwFw">The species is cultivated as an <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ornamental_plant" title="Ornamental plant" id="mwGA">ornamental</a>, and has been spread beyond its original range, including in North America.  Numerous cultivars have been developed, including the showy copper beech, <i id="mwGQ">Fagus sylvatica purpurea</i>. Although slightly toxic due to the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tannin" title="Tannin" id="mwGg">tannins</a> and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alkaloid" title="Alkaloid" id="mwGw">alkaloids</a> they contain, the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nut_(fruit)" title="Nut (fruit)" id="mwHA">nuts</a> are consumed by animals and humans. The trees are used for timber, such as the making of furniture.</p>

<meta property="mw:PageProp/toc" id="mwHQ" data-mw='{"autoGenerated":true}'/></section><section data-mw-section-id="1" id="mwHg" aria-labelledby="Description"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading2"><h2 id="Description">Description</h2><span class="mw-editsection"><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">[</span><a href="/w/index.php?title=Fagus_sylvatica&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1" title="Edit section: Description"><span>edit</span></a><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">]</span></span></div>

<p id="mwHw"><i id="mwIA">Fagus sylvatica</i> is a large <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tree" title="Tree" id="mwIQ">tree</a>, capable of reaching heights of up to <span about="#mwt20" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwIg" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"convert","href":"./Template:Convert"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"50"},"2":{"wt":"m"},"3":{"wt":"ft"},"abbr":{"wt":"off"}},"i":0}}]}'>50 metres (160 feet)</span> tall<sup about="#mwt29" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-4" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-4"}}'><a href="#cite_note-4" id="mwIw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwJA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwJQ">[</span>4<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwJg">]</span></span></a></sup> and <span about="#mwt21" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwJw" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"cvt","href":"./Template:Cvt"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"3"},"2":{"wt":"m"},"3":{"wt":"ft"},"frac":{"wt":"2"}},"i":0}}]}'>3</span><span typeof="mw:Entity" about="#mwt21"> </span><span about="#mwt21">m (10</span><span typeof="mw:Entity" about="#mwt21"> </span><span about="#mwt21">ft)</span> trunk diameter, though more typically <span about="#mwt22" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwKA" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"cvt","href":"./Template:Cvt"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"25"},"2":{"wt":"–"},"3":{"wt":"35"},"4":{"wt":"m"}},"i":0}}]}'>25–35</span><span typeof="mw:Entity" about="#mwt22"> </span><span about="#mwt22">m (82–115</span><span typeof="mw:Entity" about="#mwt22"> </span><span about="#mwt22">ft)</span> tall and up to <span about="#mwt23" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwKQ" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"cvt","href":"./Template:Cvt"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"1.5"},"2":{"wt":"m"},"3":{"wt":"ft"},"frac":{"wt":"2"}},"i":0}}]}'>1.5</span><span typeof="mw:Entity" about="#mwt23"> </span><span about="#mwt23">m (5</span><span typeof="mw:Entity" about="#mwt23"> </span><span about="#mwt23">ft)</span> trunk diameter. A 10-year-old sapling will stand about <span about="#mwt24" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwKg" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"cvt","href":"./Template:Cvt"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"4"},"2":{"wt":"m"},"3":{"wt":"ft"}},"i":0}}]}'>4</span><span typeof="mw:Entity" about="#mwt24"> </span><span about="#mwt24">m (13</span><span typeof="mw:Entity" about="#mwt24"> </span><span about="#mwt24">ft)</span> tall. Undisturbed, the European beech has a lifespan of 300 years; one tree at the Valle Cervara site was more than 500 years old, the oldest known in the northern hemisphere.<sup about="#mwt32" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-5" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-5"}}'><a href="#cite_note-5" id="mwKw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwLA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwLQ">[</span>5<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwLg">]</span></span></a></sup> In cultivated forest stands trees are normally harvested at 80–120 years of age.<sup about="#mwt35" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-6" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-6"}}'><a href="#cite_note-6" id="mwLw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwMA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwMQ">[</span>6<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwMg">]</span></span></a></sup> 30 years are needed to attain full maturity (as compared to 40 for <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fagus_grandifolia" title="Fagus grandifolia" id="mwMw">American beech</a>). Like most trees, its form depends on the location; in forest areas, <i id="mwNA">F.<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwNQ"> </span>sylvatica</i> grows to over <span about="#mwt25" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwNg" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"cvt","href":"./Template:Cvt"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"30"},"2":{"wt":"m"},"3":{"wt":"ft"},"4":{"wt":"0"}},"i":0}}]}'>30</span><span typeof="mw:Entity" about="#mwt25"> </span><span about="#mwt25">m (98</span><span typeof="mw:Entity" about="#mwt25"> </span><span about="#mwt25">ft)</span>, with branches being high up on the trunk. In open locations, it will become much shorter (typically <span about="#mwt26" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwNw" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"cvt","href":"./Template:Cvt"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"20"},"2":{"wt":"m"},"3":{"wt":"ft"},"4":{"wt":"0"}},"i":0}}]}'>20</span><span typeof="mw:Entity" about="#mwt26"> </span><span about="#mwt26">m (66</span><span typeof="mw:Entity" about="#mwt26"> </span><span about="#mwt26">ft)</span>) but more massive with a broader crown and stouter trunk.</p>

<p id="mwOA">The <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Leaves" title="Leaves" class="mw-redirect" id="mwOQ">leaves</a> are alternate, simple, and entire or with a slightly <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Crenate" title="Crenate" class="mw-redirect" id="mwOg">crenate</a> margin, <style data-mw-deduplicate="TemplateStyles:r1154941027" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles mw:Transclusion" about="#mwt36" id="mwOw" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Fraction/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"convert","href":"./Template:Convert"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"5"},"2":{"wt":"-"},"3":{"wt":"10"},"4":{"wt":"cm"},"abbr":{"wt":"on"},"frac":{"wt":"8"}},"i":0}}]}'>.mw-parser-output .frac{white-space:nowrap}.mw-parser-output .frac .num,.mw-parser-output .frac .den{font-size:80%;line-height:0;vertical-align:super}.mw-parser-output .frac .den{vertical-align:sub}.mw-parser-output .sr-only{border:0;clip:rect(0,0,0,0);clip-path:polygon(0px 0px,0px 0px,0px 0px);height:1px;margin:-1px;overflow:hidden;padding:0;position:absolute;width:1px}</style><span about="#mwt36">5–10</span><span typeof="mw:Entity" about="#mwt36"> </span><span about="#mwt36">cm (2–</span><span class="frac" about="#mwt36">3<span class="sr-only">+</span><span class="num">7</span><span typeof="mw:Entity">⁄</span><span class="den">8</span></span><span typeof="mw:Entity" about="#mwt36"> </span><span about="#mwt36">in)</span> long and <span about="#mwt38" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwPA" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"convert","href":"./Template:Convert"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"3"},"2":{"wt":"-"},"3":{"wt":"7"},"4":{"wt":"cm"},"abbr":{"wt":"on"}},"i":0}}]}'>3–7</span><span typeof="mw:Entity" about="#mwt38"> </span><span about="#mwt38">cm (1.2–2.8</span><span typeof="mw:Entity" about="#mwt38"> </span><span about="#mwt38">in)</span> broad, with 6–7 veins on each side of the leaf (as opposed to 7–10 veins in <i id="mwPQ"><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fagus_orientalis" title="Fagus orientalis" id="mwPg">F.<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwPw"> </span>orientalis</a></i>). When crenate, there is one point at each vein tip, never any points between the veins. The <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bud" title="Bud" id="mwQA">buds</a> are long and slender, <link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1154941027" about="#mwt39" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles mw:Transclusion" id="mwQQ" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Fraction/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"convert","href":"./Template:Convert"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"15"},"2":{"wt":"–"},"3":{"wt":"30"},"4":{"wt":"mm"},"frac":{"wt":"8"},"abbr":{"wt":"on"}},"i":0}}]}'/><span about="#mwt39">15–30</span><span typeof="mw:Entity" about="#mwt39"> </span><span about="#mwt39">mm (</span><span class="frac" about="#mwt39"><span class="num">5</span><span typeof="mw:Entity">⁄</span><span class="den">8</span></span><span about="#mwt39">–</span><span class="frac" about="#mwt39">1<span class="sr-only">+</span><span class="num">1</span><span typeof="mw:Entity">⁄</span><span class="den">8</span></span><span typeof="mw:Entity" about="#mwt39"> </span><span about="#mwt39">in)</span> long and <link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1154941027" about="#mwt41" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles mw:Transclusion" id="mwQg" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Fraction/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"cvt","href":"./Template:Cvt"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"2"},"2":{"wt":"–"},"3":{"wt":"3"},"4":{"wt":"mm"},"frac":{"wt":"32"}},"i":0}}]}'/><span about="#mwt41">2–3</span><span typeof="mw:Entity" about="#mwt41"> </span><span about="#mwt41">mm (</span><span class="frac" about="#mwt41"><span class="num">3</span><span typeof="mw:Entity">⁄</span><span class="den">32</span></span><span about="#mwt41">–</span><span class="frac" about="#mwt41"><span class="num">1</span><span typeof="mw:Entity">⁄</span><span class="den">8</span></span><span typeof="mw:Entity" about="#mwt41"> </span><span about="#mwt41">in)</span> thick, but thicker, up to <link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1154941027" about="#mwt43" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles mw:Transclusion" id="mwQw" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Fraction/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"convert","href":"./Template:Convert"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"4"},"2":{"wt":"–"},"3":{"wt":"5"},"4":{"wt":"mm"},"frac":{"wt":"8"},"abbr":{"wt":"on"}},"i":0}}]}'/><span about="#mwt43">4–5</span><span typeof="mw:Entity" about="#mwt43"> </span><span about="#mwt43">mm (</span><span class="frac" about="#mwt43"><span class="num">1</span><span typeof="mw:Entity">⁄</span><span class="den">8</span></span><span about="#mwt43">–</span><span class="frac" about="#mwt43"><span class="num">1</span><span typeof="mw:Entity">⁄</span><span class="den">4</span></span><span typeof="mw:Entity" about="#mwt43"> </span><span about="#mwt43">in)</span>, where the buds include flower buds.</p>

<p id="mwRA">The leaves of beech are often not <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Abscission" title="Abscission" id="mwRQ">abscissed</a> (dropped) in the autumn and instead remain on the tree until the spring. This process is called <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marcescence" title="Marcescence" id="mwRg">marcescence</a>. This particularly occurs when trees are <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sapling" title="Sapling" class="mw-redirect" id="mwRw">saplings</a> or when plants are clipped as a hedge (making beech hedges attractive screens, even in winter), but it also often continues to occur on the lower branches when the tree is mature.</p>

<p id="mwSA">The species is <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Monoecious" title="Monoecious" class="mw-redirect" id="mwSQ">monoecious</a>. The male flowers are borne in small <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Catkin" title="Catkin" id="mwSg">catkins</a>,<sup about="#mwt51" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-7" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-7"}}'><a href="#cite_note-7" id="mwSw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwTA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwTQ">[</span>7<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwTg">]</span></span></a></sup> a hallmark of the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fagales" title="Fagales" id="mwTw">Fagales</a> order. The female flowers produce beechnuts, small triangular <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nut_(fruit)" title="Nut (fruit)" id="mwUA">nuts</a> <link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1154941027" about="#mwt45" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles mw:Transclusion" id="mwUQ" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Fraction/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"cvt","href":"./Template:Cvt"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"15"},"2":{"wt":"–"},"3":{"wt":"20"},"4":{"wt":"mm"},"frac":{"wt":"8"}},"i":0}}]}'/><span about="#mwt45">15–20</span><span typeof="mw:Entity" about="#mwt45"> </span><span about="#mwt45">mm (</span><span class="frac" about="#mwt45"><span class="num">5</span><span typeof="mw:Entity">⁄</span><span class="den">8</span></span><span about="#mwt45">–</span><span class="frac" about="#mwt45"><span class="num">3</span><span typeof="mw:Entity">⁄</span><span class="den">4</span></span><span typeof="mw:Entity" about="#mwt45"> </span><span about="#mwt45">in)</span> long and <link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1154941027" about="#mwt47" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles mw:Transclusion" id="mwUg" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Fraction/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"cvt","href":"./Template:Cvt"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"7"},"2":{"wt":"–"},"3":{"wt":"10"},"4":{"wt":"mm"},"frac":{"wt":"8"}},"i":0}}]}'/><span about="#mwt47">7–10</span><span typeof="mw:Entity" about="#mwt47"> </span><span about="#mwt47">mm (</span><span class="frac" about="#mwt47"><span class="num">1</span><span typeof="mw:Entity">⁄</span><span class="den">4</span></span><span about="#mwt47">–</span><span class="frac" about="#mwt47"><span class="num">3</span><span typeof="mw:Entity">⁄</span><span class="den">8</span></span><span typeof="mw:Entity" about="#mwt47"> </span><span about="#mwt47">in)</span> wide at the base; there are two nuts in each cupule, maturing in the autumn 5–6 months after pollination. Flower and seed production is particularly abundant in years following a hot, sunny and dry summer, though rarely for two years in a row. Small quantities of seeds may be produced around 10 years of age, but not a heavy crop until the tree is at least 30 years old.</p>

<p id="mwUw">Spring leaf budding by the European beech is triggered by a combination of day length and temperature. Bud break each year is from the middle of April to the beginning of May, often with remarkable precision (within a few days). It is more precise in the north of its range than the south, and at <span about="#mwt52" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwVA" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"cvt","href":"./Template:Cvt"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"600"},"2":{"wt":"m"}},"i":0}}]}'>600</span><span typeof="mw:Entity" about="#mwt52"> </span><span about="#mwt52">m (2,000</span><span typeof="mw:Entity" about="#mwt52"> </span><span about="#mwt52">ft)</span> than at <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sea_level" title="Sea level" id="mwVQ">sea level</a>.<sup about="#mwt55" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-8" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-8"}}'><a href="#cite_note-8" id="mwVg"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwVw"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwWA">[</span>8<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwWQ">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<ul class="gallery mw-gallery-nolines center" typeof="mw:Extension/gallery" about="#mwt56" id="mwWg" data-mw='{"name":"gallery","attrs":{"class":"center","mode":"nolines","widths":"160","heights":"160"},"body":{}}'>
<li class="gallerybox" style="width: 165px;" id="mwWw"><div class="thumb" style="width: 160px;" id="mwXA"><span typeof="mw:File" id="mwXQ"><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Entzia_-_Brotes_de_haya_01.jpg" class="mw-file-description" title="Seedlings" id="mwXg"><img resource="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Entzia_-_Brotes_de_haya_01.jpg" src="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/49/Entzia_-_Brotes_de_haya_01.jpg/250px-Entzia_-_Brotes_de_haya_01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail" decoding="async" loading="lazy" srcset="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/49/Entzia_-_Brotes_de_haya_01.jpg/330px-Entzia_-_Brotes_de_haya_01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail 2x" alt="Seedlings" data-file-width="2637" data-file-height="2028" data-file-type="bitmap" height="123" width="160" class="mw-file-element" id="mwXw"/></a></span></div><div class="gallerytext" id="mwYA">Seedlings</div></li>
